  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>This catfish from Bolivia is among the smallest of its relatives, reaching a maximum of 13 cm. It is mostly imported by travelling aquarium hobbyists.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: Bolivia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>Regarding water conditions this species is rather adaptable: guide values of up to 400 µS/cm conductivity and a pH of 7.6 were measured. Approx. 25°C is quite enough for keeping and breeding.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>There are no heavy demands on aquarium decoration. It is only absolutely necessary to provide fine sand as substrate, in which these catfish can burrow.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>These fish are hardly demanding with food; a wide range of frozen and dry food is sufficient.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>In contrast to many other catfish of this family, P. thorectes is one of the kinds that only leave the protecting ground at night and after settling for food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/pseudohemiodon_thorectes_en/",
      "html": "<p>Breeding is possible, although not very productive; approx. 25 eggs can be considered a good result. Like the other Pseudohemiodon species, P. thorectes is a so-called \"lip-brooder\": the males grip the eggs with their lower lip and carry them around. After 14 days the larvae hatch and are already independent.</p>"
    }
  ],
